  "slug": "quantum-spin-96c3",
  "summary": "Recent preprints propose superconducting and Josephson devices whose design predictions target thermal noise, thermometry and spin-to-charge conversion.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is phase-coherent control of charge, spin and thermal responses by superconducting or Josephson elements. A numerical study proposes a flux-driven DC-SQUID threshold detector whose nonequilibrium phase dynamics aim to reduce thermal noise while detecting microwave pulses near the single-microwave-photon energy scale. A theory proposal for a two-orbital quantum dot Josephson junction with Rashba spin-orbit coupling and Zeeman splitting maps a temperature difference onto a phase difference for thermometry. A third preprint argues that the inverse spin Hall effect in a superconductor could approach unity spin-to-charge conversion in a nonlocal spin-valve geometry. All three items are theoretical or numerical proposals, so the new content consists of design predictions rather than experimental demonstrations.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
